      "note": "The immediate aim is to reproduce a known\nlanguage-model learning recipe. Math gives\nus an answer we can check. Recursive\nchoices come later. This is a small\nreproduction, not a new method or a\nreproduction of the paper's headline\nscores."
    },
    {
      "idx": 1,
      "label": "2",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "This example is invented. The model\ngenerates its own attempts. The checker\nsupplies only final-answer scores.\nRelative scores guide weight updates. We\nuse eight attempts per question. If all\neight scores agree, there is no within-\nquestion learning signal."
    },
    {
      "idx": 2,
      "label": "3",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "Three runs started from the original\nweights: one chat-style, two question-\nonly. Each used the same 512 training\nquestions. All 500 test questions were\nexcluded from weight training; 64 were\nmonitored. No SFT stage was added. We used\nthe final model from every run."
    },
    {
      "idx": 3,
      "label": "4",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "Across a row, weights stay fixed. Down a\ncolumn, test input stays fixed. The\nquestion-only runs gain nine and one over\nthe stronger base. The repeat has 18 gains\nand 17 losses. We have not established a\nreliable gain beyond that starting setup."
    },
    {
      "idx": 4,
      "label": "5",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "Actual separate-test row 5, summarized. Of\nits 44 gains, 36 started as capped\nresponses. No generated code was executed.\nNot every gain is necessarily format\nrepair. More training is a proposed test,\nnot a promised improvement or evidence\nabout recursive choices."
